3-TIER ARCHITECTURE


Building a highly available web application (Load Balancer + Auto Scaling + secure 3-tier architecture)

I finished a hands-on AWS lab where I transformed a single-instance application into a highly available system running across two Availability Zones. The lab took about 40 minutes and covered VPC inspection, load balancing, auto scaling, security hardening, and high availability testing.

What I built:

A public Application Load Balancer distributed across two Availability Zones

A target group with faster health checks

A custom machine image created from an existing web server

A launch configuration to deploy identical web servers automatically

An Auto Scaling Group running in private subnets across two Availability Zones

Secure 3-tier architecture using strict security group rules:

Traffic from the internet allowed only to the load balancer

Load balancer allowed to communicate only with the application layer

Application layer allowed to communicate only with the database layer

What I did:
1. Inspected the VPC

Reviewed the virtual network structure.

Analyzed public and private subnets.

Checked routing rules, internet access, and internal outbound traffic.

Audited existing security group rules and tightened access where necessary.

2. Created an Application Load Balancer

Deployed a public-facing load balancer across two Availability Zones.

Created a new security group allowing only web traffic from the internet.

Configured a target group with optimized health checks.

Connected the load balancer to the target group.

3. Created a Custom Machine Image

Took a running web server.

Created an image from it to ensure consistency across new deployments.

4. Built a Launch Configuration

Used the custom image.

Attached a secure application-layer security group.

Enabled monitoring and permissions.

Added bootstrapping scripts to:

Install required software

Deploy the web app

Start the web service on launch

5. Created an Auto Scaling Group

Deployed instances into private subnets across multiple Availability Zones.

Attached it to the target group.

Configured health checks and scaling limits.

Ensured the group always maintained two running instances.

6. Strengthened the 3-Tier Security Model

The application layer only accepts traffic from the load balancer.

The database layer only accepts traffic from the application layer.

No direct internet exposure for the app or database layers.

This design ensures secure traffic flow:
Internet → Load Balancer → Application Layer → Database Layer

7. Tested High Availability

Verified both instances became healthy behind the load balancer.

Confirmed traffic was load-balanced across Availability Zones.

Manually terminated an application instance.

Verified traffic continued without disruption.

Confirmed auto scaling replaced the failed instance automatically.

What I learned:

 How to design a highly available architecture using multiple Availability Zones
 How to secure each layer using security group references instead of IP ranges
 How load balancers and auto scaling work together for reliability
 How private subnets and NAT improve security
 How high availability works during instance failures

Why this matters

This architecture demonstrates how AWS enables fault tolerance, scalability, and strong security by design. These are core patterns used in real-world production systems to ensure applications remain online even during failures.
